      "math_academy_id": "e-483",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin^2(3x)  \\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
